# Converted to the revised ten-section template (plus section 11); content and tables carried from the previous version.
BOOK = '1 Kings'
TESTAMENT = 'Old Testament'
GENRE = 'history'
SUBTITLE = 'Solomon’s Temple, the Divided Kingdom, and the Prophet Elijah'
SECTIONS = [(1, 'Book Setting & Program Identity', [('p', 'This section introduces you to the book by explaining who wrote it, who received it, and what was happening when it was written. It also shows you which of God’s programs the book belongs to — Israel’s prophetic program or the Body of Christ’s mystery program. Knowing the setting and the program identity helps you read the book in its proper context and understand how its teachings fit into the larger story of Scripture.'), ('p', '1 Kings begins with David’s last days and Solomon’s throne. It records Solomon’s request for an understanding heart, the building and dedication of the temple, the LORD’s warning to Solomon, the division of the kingdom, the golden calves, and Elijah’s ministry against Baal worship (1 Kgs. 3:9; 6:38; 8:10–11; 9:3–5; 11:11; 12:16, 28; 18:21).'), ('box', 'Program Identity:', 'This book belongs entirely to the Prophecy Program, where the LORD dwells with Israel in the temple and Israel’s kings answer for the covenant (1 Kgs. 8:56; 9:3–5).')]),
(2, 'Narrative Flow of 1 Kings', [('p', 'This section gives you a simple walkthrough of how the book unfolds. Instead of diving into details right away, it shows the major movements in the book — how its message begins, how it develops, and how it builds toward its close. Seeing the flow of the book helps you understand the big picture before you study the individual passages.'), ('h3', 'Movement 1 — Solomon’s Throne and Wisdom (Chs. 1–4)'), ('p', 'David charges Solomon to keep the law of Moses, and Solomon asks, “Give therefore thy servant an understanding heart to judge thy people” (1 Kgs. 2:3; 3:9, 12).'), ('h3', 'Movement 2 — The Temple (Chs. 5–8)'), ('p', 'The house is finished in seven years, the glory of the LORD fills it, and Solomon blesses the LORD for His promise to Israel (1 Kgs. 6:38; 8:10–11, 23, 27, 56, 60).'), ('h3', 'Movement 3 — Warning and Division (Chs. 9–12)'), ('p', 'The LORD answers Solomon’s prayer, sets conditions on the throne, rends the kingdom after Solomon’s idolatry, and Israel departs from David’s house (1 Kgs. 9:3–5; 11:4, 11, 36; 12:16, 28).'), ('h3', 'Movement 4 — Elijah and Ahab (Chs. 13–22)'), ('p', 'Elijah speaks to Ahab, calls Israel to choose between the LORD and Baal, and hears a still small voice at Horeb (1 Kgs. 17:1, 24; 18:21, 39; 19:12, 18).')]),
(3, 'Program Themes in 1 Kings', [('p', 'This section highlights the major ideas emphasized in the book — the big doctrinal themes that shape how Israel should think and live under the covenants. These themes are not verse-by-verse explanations; they are the book’s main emphases, the repeated ideas God wants Israel to carry forward. Seeing these themes together helps you understand the book’s message before you look at the individual truths in the tables.'), ('ol', ['<b>The Temple Filled with Glory</b> — “The glory of the LORD had filled the house of the LORD” (1 Kgs. 8:11).', '<b>The Promise Kept</b> — “There hath not failed one word of all his good promise, which he promised by the hand of Moses his servant” (1 Kgs. 8:56).', '<b>The Throne Conditioned</b> — “Then I will establish the throne of thy kingdom upon Israel for ever, as I promised to David thy father” (1 Kgs. 9:5).', '<b>The Kingdom Rent</b> — “I will surely rend the kingdom from thee, and will give it to thy servant” (1 Kgs. 11:11).', '<b>A Light for David</b> — “That David my servant may have a light alway before me in Jerusalem, the city which I have chosen me to put my name there” (1 Kgs. 11:36).', '<b>The LORD or Baal</b> — “If the LORD be God, follow him: but if Baal, then follow him” (1 Kgs. 18:21).'])]),
(4, 'Christ in 1 Kings', [('p', 'This section gathers what the book teaches about Christ — through foreshadowing, typology, and prophetic anticipation. These statements help you see the book’s Christ-centered trajectory before you study the doctrinal truths in detail. By collecting these points together, you get a clear picture of how 1 Kings points to Christ.'), ('ol', ['<b>The Son of David</b> — Solomon builds the house and the LORD promises the throne to David’s line (1 Kgs. 6:38; 9:5), within Israel’s kingdom hope.', '<b>The Temple and the Glory</b> — The glory of the LORD fills the house (1 Kgs. 8:11); the book reports this as written.', '<b>The Remnant</b> — The LORD says, “I have left me seven thousand in Israel” who have not bowed to Baal (1 Kgs. 19:18).'])]),
(5, 'Israel, the Law, and the Covenants in 1 Kings', [('p', 'This section helps you see how 1 Kings speaks about Israel, the law, and the covenants without mixing programs. 1 Kings operates within Israel’s prophetic framework. By gathering these points together, this section shows you how to read 1 Kings in its own setting — as an Israel-program book — while recognizing what it says (and does not say) about the Body of Christ.'), ('p', '1 Kings reinforces Israel’s:'), ('ul', ['temple and glory of the LORD (1 Kgs. 8:10–11)', 'promise to David’s throne (1 Kgs. 9:5)', 'the divided kingdom (1 Kgs. 12:16)', 'prophets sent to Israel (1 Kgs. 17:1)', 'a remnant that has not bowed to Baal (1 Kgs. 19:18)']), ('p', 'All content belongs exclusively to the Prophecy Program.')]),
(6, 'Israel-Program Distinctions', [('p', 'This section highlights the teachings in 1 Kings that belong uniquely to Israel’s prophetic program — truths rooted in prophecy, covenant promises, national identity, and God’s dealings with Israel. By gathering these together, this section helps you see how 1 Kings contributes to Israel’s story without borrowing from the Body-of-Christ program.'), ('ul', ['The Law of Moses Kept — “as it is written in the law of Moses.” (1 Kgs. 2:3).', 'The Temple Finished — “in the eleventh year… was the house finished throughout all the parts thereof.” (1 Kgs. 6:38).', 'The Glory Fills the House — “the glory of the LORD had filled the house of the LORD.” (1 Kgs. 8:11).', 'The Promise Not Failed — “there hath not failed one word of all his good promise.” (1 Kgs. 8:56).', 'The Throne Over Israel — “I will establish the throne of thy kingdom upon Israel for ever.” (1 Kgs. 9:5).', 'The Kingdom Rent — “I will surely rend the kingdom from thee.” (1 Kgs. 11:11).', 'A Light for David — “that David my servant may have a light alway before me in Jerusalem.” (1 Kgs. 11:36).', 'The Calves of Gold — “made two calves of gold.” (1 Kgs. 12:28).', 'The Seven Thousand — “I have left me seven thousand in Israel.” (1 Kgs. 19:18).'])]),
(7, 'Truths Table: What 1 Kings Says', [('p', 'This table gathers the key statements, instructions, and teachings in 1 Kings and shows which ones belong to Israel’s prophetic program. Each row lists a truth from the book, explains what it says, and identifies its program identity based on Israel’s covenant setting. This helps you see, at a glance, what 1 Kings teaches directly to Israel and keeps those truths separate from universal principles that apply more broadly.'), ('table', ['Category', 'Truth Revealed in 1 Kings', 'Program Identity', 'Scripture'], [['The Law of Moses Kept', '“as it is written in the law of Moses.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', '1 Kgs. 2:3'], ['The Temple Finished', '“in the eleventh year… was the house finished throughout all the parts thereof.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', '1 Kgs. 6:38'], ['The Glory Fills the House', '“the glory of the LORD had filled the house of the LORD.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', '1 Kgs. 8:11'], ['The Promise Not Failed', '“there hath not failed one word of all his good promise.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', '1 Kgs. 8:56'], ['The Throne Over Israel', '“I will establish the throne of thy kingdom upon Israel for ever.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', '1 Kgs. 9:5'], ['The Kingdom Rent', '“I will surely rend the kingdom from thee.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', '1 Kgs. 11:11'], ['A Light for David', '“that David my servant may have a light alway before me in Jerusalem.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', '1 Kgs. 11:36'], ['The Calves of Gold', '“made two calves of gold.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', '1 Kgs. 12:28'], ['The Seven Thousand', '“I have left me seven thousand in Israel.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', '1 Kgs. 19:18']], [20, 34, 26, 20])]),
(8, 'Universal Truths Table (All People, All Time)', [('p', 'This table gathers the truths in 1 Kings that apply to everyone, not just Israel. These are principles rooted in God’s character — things that hold true across all programs and all ages. By separating these universal truths from Israel’s covenant-specific instructions, this section helps you see what 1 Kings teaches all believers everywhere and what it teaches Israel in its own setting.'), ('table', ['Universal Truth', 'Description', 'Scripture'], [['Understanding for Judging', '“Give therefore thy servant an understanding heart to judge thy people.”', '1 Kgs. 3:9'], ['No God Like Him', '“LORD God of Israel, there is no God like thee, in heaven above, or on earth beneath.”', '1 Kgs. 8:23'], ['The Heavens Cannot Contain Him', '“the heaven and heaven of heavens cannot contain thee.”', '1 Kgs. 8:27'], ['That All May Know', '“That all the people of the earth may know that the LORD is God, and that there is none else.”', '1 Kgs. 8:60'], ['The Still Small Voice', '“after the fire a still small voice.”', '1 Kgs. 19:12']], [28, 48, 24])]),
(9, 'Program-Safe Application Grid', [('p', 'This section shows how to apply the teachings of 1 Kings without mixing programs. The applications come from Israel’s covenant story and from universal truths that apply to everyone. By keeping the applications tied to what the book actually says — and not borrowing commands from Israel’s covenants for the Body of Christ — this grid helps you respond to 1 Kings in a way that is faithful to its setting, clear, and safe.'), ('ol', ['<b>Ask God for wisdom</b> — An understanding heart (1 Kgs. 3:9).', '<b>Confess that there is no God like Him</b> (1 Kgs. 8:23).', '<b>Remember He cannot be contained</b> (1 Kgs. 8:27).', '<b>Wish all peoples to know the LORD</b> (1 Kgs. 8:60).', '<b>Listen for His voice</b> — A still small voice (1 Kgs. 19:12).'])]),
(10, 'Teaching Outline for 1 Kings', [('p', 'This outline gives you a teacher-ready structure for presenting the book. It follows the narrative movements and highlights the major themes, making it easy to teach 1 Kings in classes, small groups, or personal study.'), ('olr', ['Solomon’s Throne (Chs. 1–4) — Wisdom asked. <i>(1 Kgs. 2:3; 3:9, 12)</i>', 'The Temple (Chs. 5–8) — The glory fills the house. <i>(1 Kgs. 6:38; 8:10–11, 56)</i>', 'Warning and Division (Chs. 9–12) — The kingdom rent. <i>(1 Kgs. 9:5; 11:11, 36; 12:16, 28)</i>', 'Elijah (Chs. 13–22) — The LORD or Baal. <i>(1 Kgs. 18:21, 39; 19:12, 18)</i>'])]),
(11, 'Companion Usage and Study Suggestions', [('h3', 'How to Use This Companion in Personal Study'), ('p', 'This Companion is designed to walk alongside you as you read 1 Kings, helping you see both the prophetic structure of the book and the timeless truths rooted in God’s character. Use it slowly, chapter by chapter, letting the narrative movements, themes, and universal truths deepen your understanding. The Program-Safe Applications will guide your heart toward wisdom, faithfulness, and trust without confusing Israel’s covenant commands with the Body of Christ’s calling today.'), ('h3', 'How to Use This Companion in Small Groups or Classes'), ('p', 'This Companion provides a clear, structured framework that makes teaching 1 Kings simple and accessible. Each section gives you ready-to-use material: narrative movements for overview, prophecy-program themes for doctrinal clarity, universal truths for discussion, and a pastoral teaching outline for weekly lessons. Use the tables to help your group distinguish Israel’s covenant identity from universal principles, and use the Program-Safe Application Grid to foster meaningful, Christ-centered conversation.'), ('h3', 'How This Companion Fits Within the Acts Overlap Theology Series'), ('p', 'This Companion is part of a larger series designed to help believers read every book of Scripture without blurring God’s prophetic program for Israel and His mystery program for the Body of Christ. Within the Acts Overlap Theology Series, 1 Kings stands firmly in Israel’s covenant story, yet it still reveals universal truths that shape Christian character today. This Companion shows you how to honor the book’s prophetic context while drawing out the timeless principles that apply to all believers in every age.')])]
